const e=[{id:"l-formation",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Formation: the moment a contract exists",minutes:25,summary:"Section 4 of the Contracts Act fixes a different moment for each kind of message, and section 5 decides the race between an acceptance and a withdrawal.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-formation-01"],reading:[{bookId:"cheong-contract-law-malaysia",where:"the chapters on offer and acceptance"},{bookId:"sinnadurai-law-of-contract",where:"for the fuller treatment and the case law"}],source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136) ss 2(a), 4, 5, 6; Macon Works & Trading Sdn Bhd v Phang Hon Chin [1976] 2 MLJ 177.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read ss 4 and 5 in a current reprint of Act 136 and check the subsection lettering against your text before quoting it. Confirm the Macon Works citation.",plate:"letters",plateCaption:"Two letters crossing. Section 4 fixes a different moment for each of them, and the race is decided by which moment applies.",readMinutes:11,sections:[{id:"sec-l-formation-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A room, a message, and a second tenant — whether there is a contract turns on one moment.",h:"The moment that decides it",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Raju advertises a room in Cheras at RM450 a month. Mei Ling messages him on Tuesday to say that she will take it. On Wednesday he tells her the room has gone to somebody else."},{t:"p",text:"Whether Mei Ling has a contract depends on the exact moment one came into being, if it ever did. Malaysian law fixes that moment by statute, and the statute is precise about it."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Malaysian contract law is a statute, and it does not always match the English cases.",h:"A statute, not the common law",body:[{t:"p",text:"Malaysian contract law is written down in a statute. The Contracts Act 1950 sets out the rules of formation, and those rules do not always match the English ones that a common law textbook will give you."},{t:"p",text:"Copying English contract law across without checking the Act is the most reliable way to get a Malaysian contract question wrong. Formation is the clearest example, because the Act writes down the posting rules in a form that produces different answers from the English cases."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 2(a) defines a proposal; acceptance turns it into a promise.",h:"Proposal and acceptance",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 2(a): when one person signifies to another his willingness to do or to abstain from doing anything, with a view to obtaining the assent of that other, he is said to make a proposal.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 2(a)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: a proposal is telling somebody that you are willing to do something, in order to get their agreement to it."},{t:"p",text:"The Act says **proposal** where an English book says offer, and **promise** where it says acceptance. The ideas are the same, and the sensible course is to use the vocabulary of the statute you are working in."},{t:"p",text:"A proposal has to be told apart from an **invitation to treat**, which is a signal that you are open to receiving proposals rather than a proposal itself. Goods displayed with a price, a newspaper advertisement and a notice of an auction are the standard examples. That distinction comes from the case law rather than from the Act itself."},{t:"example",text:"Encik Raju's advertisement for the room is an invitation to treat, not a proposal. Mei Ling's message saying that she will take it is the proposal. Raju has to accept that proposal before either of them is bound to anything."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-3",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 4 fixes a different moment for each kind of message, and delivery is not knowledge.",anchor:"fig-l-formation-0",h:"When a message counts",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 4 is the provision that decides most formation problems. The point to notice is that it fixes a different moment for each kind of message, and a different moment again depending on which side is relying on it. There is no single rule here to reach for."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 4(1): communication of a proposal is complete when it comes to the knowledge of the person to whom it is made.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 4(1)"},{t:"predict",prompt:"Mei Ling posts her acceptance on Monday. It reaches Encik Raju on Thursday, and he reads it that evening. Before you read on — from which day is Raju bound?",options:["Monday, when she put the letter into the post","Thursday, when he read it and knew about it","Neither — a letter binds nobody until both sides confirm"],answer:0,reveal:"Monday. It feels wrong that a man can be bound by a letter he has not opened, and that feeling is not silly. The Act is lopsided here on purpose. Sending is enough against the proposer, and only against him. Mei Ling herself is not bound until Thursday, when Raju knows. One posting, two different moments."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 4(2): communication of an acceptance is complete — (a) as against the proposer, when it is put in a course of transmission to him, so as to be out of the power of the acceptor; and (b) as against the acceptor, when it comes to the knowledge of the proposer.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 4(2)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: sending the acceptance binds the proposer straight away, but it does not bind the acceptor until the proposer knows about it."},{t:"p",text:"A **revocation** is a withdrawal — the proposer taking his offer back before it has been accepted. Section 4(3) gives a revocation the same two-sided treatment as an acceptance."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 4(3): communication of a revocation is complete — (a) as against the person who makes it, when it is put into a course of transmission; and (b) as against the person to whom it is made, when it comes to his knowledge.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 4(3)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: sending a withdrawal commits the sender straight away, but it does nothing at all to the other side until that side actually knows of it."},{t:"p",text:"Read those provisions together and a pattern appears. Sending is enough to bind the person the message is aimed at in exactly one case: an acceptance, as against the proposer. Everywhere else the Act asks for knowledge, and that lopsidedness is deliberate."},{t:"table",caption:"The four moments section 4 fixes",columns:[{label:"Communication"},{label:"Complete as against the maker"},{label:"Complete as against the other party"}],rows:[["Proposal — s 4(1)","—","When it comes to that person's knowledge"],["Acceptance — s 4(2)","When put into a course of transmission, out of the acceptor's power","When it comes to the proposer's knowledge"],["Revocation — s 4(3)","When put into a course of transmission","When it comes to that person's knowledge"]],note:"The trap is the last cell. **Delivery is not knowledge** — an unopened letter on a desk has been delivered and not communicated, and a problem that mentions one is testing exactly that.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 4"}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-4",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A proposal can be revoked until acceptance is complete against the proposer — which is why the moment matters.",h:"The race",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 5(1): a proposal may be revoked at any time before the communication of its acceptance is complete as against the proposer, but not afterwards.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 5(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put section 5(1) beside section 4(2)(a) and the deadline becomes exact. The proposer may take his offer back until the moment the acceptance is sent, and not one minute after that."},{t:"p",text:"Put it beside section 4(3)(b) and the other half of the race appears. His withdrawal does not touch the acceptor until the acceptor actually knows about it."},{t:"example",text:"Raju posts an offer to Mei Ling on 3 March. On 10 March he changes his mind and posts a letter withdrawing it. That letter is delivered to Mei Ling's office on the afternoon of 12 March, but she does not open it until 14 March. She had already posted her acceptance at nine in the morning on 12 March. That acceptance bound Raju the moment it went into the postbox. The withdrawal reached Mei Ling only on 14 March, when she read it, so it was too late and there is a contract."},{t:"caution",text:"Delivery is not the same thing as knowledge. Section 4(3)(b) asks for knowledge, and a letter lying unopened on a desk has not been communicated to anybody at all. This is the step that most answers skip over."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Raju's letter withdrawing his offer is delivered to Mei Ling's office on Monday. She opens it on Wednesday. When is the withdrawal complete against her?",options:["Monday — the letter was in her hands","Wednesday — that is when she knew of it","The day Raju posted it"],answer:1,why:"Section 4(3)(b) asks for knowledge, and an unopened letter on a desk is not knowledge. The first answer is the step most answers skip: delivery is not communication. The third is the right rule read off the wrong half of the subsection. Posting binds Raju, and it does nothing to Mei Ling."},{t:"p",text:"Section 6 lists the ways a proposal comes to an end. They are notice of revocation, lapse of the time allowed, and failure to fulfil a condition. A proposal also ends on the death or mental disorder of the proposer, where the acceptor knows of it before accepting."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A promise to hold an offer open binds nobody without consideration.",h:"An offer held open",body:[{t:"p",text:"Raju tells Mei Ling that the room is hers if she decides within fourteen days. Three days later he lets it to somebody else instead. Was he allowed to do that?"},{t:"p",text:"He was, unless she gave him something for the promise to wait. A promise to keep an offer open is itself a promise, and a promise that is not paid for does not bind. What each side gives in return for the other's promise is called **consideration**."},{t:"rule",text:"A promise to keep an offer open for a stated period is not binding unless supported by consideration; the proposer may revoke within the period.",source:"Macon Works & Trading Sdn Bhd v Phang Hon Chin [1976] 2 MLJ 177"},{t:"caution",text:"It is easy to over-apply this rule. All it means is that the proposer *could* have revoked in time, not that he actually did so. Check the timing under sections 4 and 5 before concluding either way."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Two letters crossing in the post, and both sides arguing something wrong.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"The problem question is two letters crossing in the post, with both sides advancing an argument that is wrong."},{t:"p",text:"Working it properly means applying a different subsection to each message, rather than reaching for one rule and using it everywhere."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qfm1",q:"When is communication of a proposal complete?",options:["When it is posted","When it is signed","When it is written","When it reaches the knowledge of the person it is made to"],answer:3,why:"Section 4(1). Each kind of message has its own moment, so read the right subsection.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 4(1)"},{id:"qfm2",q:"When is an acceptance communicated, as against the proposer?",options:["When it reaches the proposer","When it is put in a course of transmission and is out of the acceptor's power","When the proposer reads it","When the acceptor makes up her mind"],answer:1,why:"Section 4(2)(a). As against the acceptor it is complete only when it comes to the proposer's knowledge.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 4(2)"},{id:"qfm3",q:"Until when may a proposal be taken back?",options:["Until communication of the acceptance is complete as against the proposer","Until the acceptance is received","At any time before the contract is performed","Until the date the offer says it expires"],answer:0,why:"Section 5(1), which is why the moment fixed by section 4(2)(a) decides the race.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 5(1)"},{id:"qfm4",q:"A letter withdrawing an offer sits unopened on the offeree's desk. Has it been communicated to her?",options:["Yes, on delivery","Yes, on posting","Only if it was sent by registered post","No — section 4(3)(b) asks for knowledge"],answer:3,why:"Delivery is not knowledge, and this is the step most answers get wrong.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 4(3)(b)"},{id:"qfm5",q:"A proposer promises to keep an offer open for fourteen days. Is that promise binding?",options:["Yes, always","Not unless something was given for it","Yes, if it is in writing","Yes, if the other side relied on it"],answer:1,why:"It means he could have revoked in time, not that he did. Check the timing under sections 4 and 5 first.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 4, 5 and 26"}]},{id:"l-consideration",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Consideration under a statute, not the common law",minutes:25,summary:"Section 2(d) differs from English law in two ways that decide cases, and section 26 rescues three promises that would otherwise fail.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-consideration-01"],reading:[{bookId:"cheong-contract-law-malaysia",where:"the chapter on consideration"},{bookId:"sinnadurai-law-of-contract",where:"on s 26 and the exceptions in detail"}],source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136) ss 2(d), 26 and Explanation 2; Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt [1968] 1 MLJ 170 (PC).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read s 26 and its exceptions in a current reprint of Act 136 — the registration requirement in (a) and the exact wording of (c) are what answers turn on. Read Kepong Prospecting itself rather than a summary, since it is cited for both consideration and privity and the two are easy to run together.",plate:"exchange",plateCaption:"The exchange, and the third figure at the table — under section 2(d) consideration may move from a person who is not the promisee.",readMinutes:11,glossaryDense:"The section 26 rule block recites the statute's three saved promises nearly verbatim, and a statutory list is made of terms of art. Fifteen marks in ninety-seven words is the provision's density, not the glossary's.",sections:[{id:"sec-l-consideration-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A promise to give money, freely made and then withdrawn, is usually unenforceable — and that surprises people.",h:"Why a bare promise fails",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Rohana tells her nephew that she will give him RM3,000 because she is proud of him. Two months later she changes her mind and says nothing more about it. Can he sue her for the money?"},{t:"predict",prompt:"Puan Rohana promised her nephew RM3,000 because she was proud of him. Then she changed her mind. Can he sue her for it?",options:["Yes — she said it plainly and she meant it","No — he gave her nothing in return","Only if she had put it in writing"],answer:1,reveal:"The second one. If you picked the first, you are holding the ordinary moral view of a promise, and the law is about to part company with it. It asks a narrower question: what did the other side give? Writing alone will not rescue a bare promise, though writing matters later in this lesson."},{t:"p",text:"Usually he cannot. A promise on its own is not a contract, and Malaysian law asks what the other side gave in return for it. What the other side gives is called **consideration**, and it is the price of a promise."},{t:"p",text:"This is the topic where borrowing English law does the most damage. The English rules are famous, and the Malaysian definition is statutory and different. Two of those differences decide ordinary cases, and both of them sit in a single sentence of the Act."}]},{id:"sec-l-consideration-1",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 2(d) is wider than English law: past consideration counts, and it may move from a third party.",h:"The definition",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 2(d): when, at the desire of the promisor, the promisee or any other person has done or abstained from doing, or does or abstains from doing, or promises to do or to abstain from doing, something, such act or abstinence or promise is called a consideration for the promise.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 2(d)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: consideration is whatever the other side does, does not do, or promises, because you asked for it. It may be something already finished, and it may come from a third person."},{t:"p",text:"Two phrases in that section should stand out, because each of them departs from English law."},{t:"list",items:["“Has done or abstained from doing” — an act already finished can count. The English rule that past consideration is no consideration does not apply here.","“The promisee or any other person” — the consideration need not come from the person the promise was made to, because a stranger to the promise may supply it."]},{t:"p",text:"Kepong Prospecting is the case usually cited for the first point. The Privy Council there treated services rendered before the agreement as good consideration under section 2(d)."},{t:"rule",text:"Past consideration is good consideration in Malaysia, and consideration need not move from the promisee.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 2(d); Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt [1968] 1 MLJ 170 (PC)"},{t:"example",text:"In June Pak Samad asks Ah Meng to repair his fence, and says nothing at all about paying him. Ah Meng goes ahead and does the work. In July Pak Samad promises him RM300 for it. The work was finished before the promise, but it was done at Pak Samad's request, so under section 2(d) it can be good consideration."},{t:"caution",text:"This does not abolish the doctrine of privity. **Privity of contract** means that only a party to a contract can sue on it. Consideration may come from a stranger, but a stranger still cannot bring the claim. Keep the two rules apart, because Kepong Prospecting gets cited for both and they are easily run together."},{t:"p",text:"The words “at the desire of the promisor” remain a real requirement. An act nobody asked for, done out of ordinary kindness, is not obviously consideration even though it is past. That is the point at which section 26 becomes the safer argument to run."}]},{id:"sec-l-consideration-2",readMinutes:5,keypoint:"Section 26 voids a bare promise, then saves three of them — and limb (a) needs writing AND registration.",anchor:"fig-l-consideration-0",h:"Section 26 and its three saves",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 26: an agreement made without consideration is void, unless — (a) it is in writing and registered, and made on account of natural love and affection between parties standing in a near relation to each other; (b) it is a promise to compensate, wholly or in part, a person who has already voluntarily done something for the promisor; or (c) it is a promise, in writing and signed by the person to be charged, to pay wholly or in part a debt of which the creditor might have enforced payment but for the law of limitation.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 26"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: no consideration, no contract — with three escapes, and each escape has a form to follow."},{t:"list",items:["(a) It is in writing and registered, and made on account of natural love and affection between parties standing in a near relation to each other.","(b) It is a promise to compensate, wholly or in part, a person who has already voluntarily done something for the promisor.","(c) It is a promise in writing, signed by the person to be charged, to pay a debt the creditor could have enforced but for the law of limitation."]},{t:"p",text:"**Limitation** is the period within which an action must be brought. Once that period has run out the debt is still owed, but the courts will no longer enforce it."},{t:"p",text:"Each exception carries its own formality, and it is usually the formality rather than the substance that decides the case."},{t:"list",items:["Limb (a) needs writing *and* registration, which is a demanding pair. Writing on its own is not enough, and this is the limb most often stated only halfway.","Limb (b) needs the earlier act to have been voluntary and done for the promisor. It is the safe route for the good turn that nobody asked for.","Limb (c) needs writing signed by the person who is to pay, and a debt that is time-barred rather than merely unpaid."]},{t:"example",text:"Ah Meng finds Puan Rohana's cat and brings it home, without being asked to. A week later she signs a note promising him RM500 for his trouble. Section 2(d) is awkward here, because nobody had asked him to do anything at all. Section 26(b) fits squarely, since it covers a promise to compensate a person who has already voluntarily done something for the promisor. That is the limb to argue first."},{t:"example",text:"A father promises his daughter RM20,000 out of natural love and affection when she finishes her degree. They are plainly in a near relation, and the love and affection is expressly the reason. But nothing was put in writing and nothing was registered, so section 26(a) is not satisfied and the promise is void."},{t:"caution",text:"Failing that second promise for want of an intention to create legal relations gives the right outcome for the wrong reason. The Act hands you a clear formality, so reach for the provision rather than for a general idea."},{t:"table",caption:"Section 26: the three saved promises",columns:[{label:"Limb"},{label:"What it requires"},{label:"The trap"}],rows:[["26(a)","Writing **and** registration; natural love and affection; parties in a near relation","Writing alone is not enough — this is the limb most often stated incompletely"],["26(b)","A promise to compensate a past act that was voluntary and done for the promisor","The act must have been voluntary, and done *for the promisor*"],["26(c)","A written, signed promise to pay a debt barred by limitation","The debt must actually be time-barred, not merely unpaid"]],source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 26"}]},{id:"sec-l-consideration-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Inadequate consideration does not void an agreement, though it may bear on whether consent was free.",h:"Is the price too small?",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Rohana sells her motorbike to her cousin for RM100 when the bike is plainly worth RM4,000. Is the sale void because the price is absurd?"},{t:"rule",text:"Explanation 2 to s 26: an agreement is not void merely because the consideration is inadequate, but inadequacy may be taken into account in determining whether the promisor's consent was freely given.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 26, Explanation 2"},{t:"p",text:"So the courts do not police whether a bargain was a good one. A wildly one-sided bargain is, however, evidence that something went wrong in the making of it — pressure, perhaps, or a misunderstanding."}]},{id:"sec-l-consideration-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Three promises, and a colleague who has advised that all three fail out of an English textbook.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"The problem question gives you three promises and a junior colleague who has advised that all three of them fail. His grounds are taken straight out of an English textbook."},{t:"p",text:"Two of the three promises actually succeed. The exercise is noticing which of his propositions is English law that the Malaysian Act has displaced."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qcs1",q:"How does section 2(d) differ from English law?",options:["Consideration must be adequate","Consideration must be in writing","Past consideration is good, and consideration need not come from the promisee","Consideration must come from the promisee alone"],answer:2,why:"Both differences decide cases, and both come straight from the words of the section.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 2(d)"},{id:"qcs2",q:"Does section 2(d) mean a stranger to a contract can sue on it?",options:["Yes","No — a stranger to the contract still cannot sue on it","Yes, for written contracts","Only where the consideration came from the stranger"],answer:1,why:"Consideration may come from a stranger. Who may sue is a separate question, and the answer is privity.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 2(d); Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt [1968] 1 MLJ 170 (PC)"},{id:"qcs3",q:"What does section 26(a) require?",options:["Writing and registration, natural love and affection, and parties in a near relation","Writing alone","Registration alone","Natural love and affection alone"],answer:0,why:"Writing on its own is not enough. This is the limb most often stated only halfway.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 26(a)"},{id:"qcs4",q:"What does Explanation 2 to section 26 say?",options:["Consideration must be adequate","Inadequate consideration is no consideration at all","Inadequate consideration does not make an agreement void, but it may bear on free consent","Adequacy is entirely for the parties"],answer:2,why:"It is the bridge between consideration and the provisions on free consent.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 26, Explanation 2"},{id:"qcs5",q:"A promise is made in return for a past voluntary act done for the promisor. Which limb saves it?",options:["Section 26(a)","Section 26(c)","None — it fails","Section 26(b)"],answer:3,why:"The act must have been voluntary and done for the promisor. That is the safe route where nobody asked for it.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 26(b)"}]},{id:"l-vitiating",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"When an agreement can be undone: the vitiating factors",minutes:30,summary:"Free consent is defined by the five things that spoil it. Each has its own section in the Contracts Act 1950, and they do not all have the same consequence.",plants:["ms-vit-01","ms-vit-02","ms-vit-03","ms-vit-04","ms-vit-05"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"cheong-contract-law-malaysia",where:"the chapters on free consent and on void and voidable agreements"}],source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 10, 11, 14-24, 66; Age of Majority Act 1971.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check each section against the current consolidated text of Act 136 — the numbering above is the traditional numbering and should be confirmed. The Malaysian position on a minor's contracts, in particular, should be checked against current authority rather than assumed from the English rules.",plate:"broken-seal",plateCaption:"A deed with the seal broken. Voidable means the agreement stands until the party entitled to avoid it says otherwise.",readMinutes:11,glossaryDense:"Five vitiating factors, each with a statutory definition narrower than the everyday word, in one lesson. The marks are the point of it.",sections:[{id:"sec-l-vitiating-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A contract needs free consent, and section 14 defines that by what removes it.",h:"Consent that was not free",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Aini signs a paper agreeing to sell her land for RM30,000. She signs because the buyer told her the land floods every year and is worth nothing. It does not flood. She agreed, but she did not agree with her eyes open."},{t:"p",text:"The law has a list of the things that spoil consent. It is a closed list, and it is in the Contracts Act 1950. Start with what a contract needs in the first place."},{t:"rule",text:"An agreement is a contract if it is made by the free consent of parties competent to contract, for a lawful consideration and with a lawful object, and is not expressly declared to be void.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 10(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: four things are needed. Consent that is free, parties old enough and of sound mind, a lawful price and a lawful purpose, and no provision declaring the agreement void."},{t:"p",text:"Consideration is the price of a promise — what each side gives in exchange for what it gets. That leaves the phrase in the middle, free consent, which the next section defines."},{t:"rule",text:"Consent is said to be free when it is not caused by coercion, undue influence, fraud, misrepresentation, or mistake.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 14"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: consent is free unless one of five named things caused it. Lawyers call those five the **vitiating factors**, from a word that means to spoil or impair."},{t:"caution",text:"Section 14 is a closed list, and ss 15 to 23 supply the statutory definitions. An answer that reasons from English duress, or from English misrepresentation, has started in the wrong place. It usually gets the consequence wrong as well."}]},{id:"sec-l-vitiating-1",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Coercion, undue influence, fraud, misrepresentation, mistake — each with a statutory definition narrower than the everyday word.",h:"The five, section by section",body:[{t:"list",items:["**Coercion (s 15)** — committing or threatening to commit an act forbidden by the Penal Code. Or unlawfully detaining property, or threatening to detain it, to the prejudice of any person. It must be done with the intention of causing a person to enter into the agreement. Notice how much narrower that is than 'pressure'.","**Undue influence (s 16)** — the relations between the parties are such that one can dominate the will of the other, and he uses that position to obtain an unfair advantage. Section 16(2) gives instances of such a position. Where the transaction appears unconscionable, s 16(3) puts it on the dominant party to show the contract was not induced by undue influence.","**Fraud (s 17)** — suggesting a fact is true when you do not believe it to be true. Also active concealment, and a promise made with no intention of performing it. Mere silence is not fraud, unless there is a duty to speak, or the silence is in itself equivalent to speech.","**Misrepresentation (s 18)** — a positive assertion, not warranted by the information the maker has, of something untrue that he believes to be true. It also covers certain breaches of duty, and causing a party to make a mistake about the subject matter. The difference from fraud is honesty.","**Mistake (ss 21 to 23)** — a mistake by both parties as to a matter of fact essential to the agreement makes it void. A mistake as to a law in force in Malaysia does not."]},{t:"example",text:"Pak Hamid is eighty and unwell. His nephew handles his bank account, his papers and his medicine. The nephew has him sign a transfer of the house for RM1. The relationship is the point: the nephew was in a position to dominate his uncle's will, and the transaction appears unconscionable. Section 16(3) then puts it on the nephew to show the transfer was not induced by undue influence."},{t:"caution",text:"Unconscionable is not the same as unwise. People are entitled to make bad bargains, and the courts do not rewrite them. The word points at a transaction so one-sided that it calls for an explanation."}]},{id:"sec-l-vitiating-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Coercion, fraud and misrepresentation make an agreement voidable; a shared mistake of essential fact makes it void.",h:"Void and voidable",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two words do a great deal of work here and they are easy to confuse."},{t:"p",text:"**Void** means the agreement had no legal effect from the beginning. There is nothing there to keep. **Voidable** means the agreement is valid and binding until the innocent party elects to undo it."},{t:"p",text:"Which one you get depends on which vitiating factor you are dealing with."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Aini signed because the buyer told her the land floods. It does not flood. Is there a contract at all?",options:["No — a lie wipes the agreement out from the start","Yes, and it binds her until she chooses to undo it","Yes, and the only thing she can ask for is money"],answer:1,reveal:"There is a contract. Coercion, fraud and misrepresentation make an agreement voidable rather than void, so it stands until the wronged party takes it down. If you picked the first answer, that instinct is not wasted. It is right about a mistake made by both parties, which section 21 does make void."},{t:"rule",text:"When consent to an agreement is caused by coercion, fraud or misrepresentation, the agreement is a contract voidable at the option of the party whose consent was so caused.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 19(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: coercion, fraud and misrepresentation give the wronged party a choice. The contract stands unless and until that party takes it down."},{t:"p",text:"She may rescind it — unwind it and put both sides back where they started. Or she may insist that the contract be performed, and that she be put in the position she would have been in if what she was told had been true."},{t:"p",text:"The right to rescind can be lost. It goes if she affirms the contract after learning the truth, if she waits too long, or if restitution has become impossible."},{t:"p",text:"A mistake by both parties about an essential fact is a different case. Section 21 makes that agreement void. There is no election to make, because there is nothing to affirm."},{t:"p",text:"Section 66 then deals with restoration. A person who has received an advantage under an agreement discovered to be void must restore it, or make compensation for it. The same goes for a contract that becomes void. It does the work an English lawyer would look for in restitution."},{t:"example",text:"Aini learns that the flood story was false. She may rescind, hand back the price and take her land. Or she may hold the buyer to the sale on the footing that what he said was true. What she may not do is wait two years, spend the money, and then rescind."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Which factor you have decides which word you get",caption:"The practical difference is the election. Voidable leaves the choice with the wronged party; void leaves nobody anything to elect about.",alt:"A question — which vitiating factor caused the consent? — branching two ways. Coercion, fraud or misrepresentation under section 19: the agreement is voidable, and the wronged party may rescind or may affirm and insist on performance. A mistake by both parties as to an essential fact falls under section 21. That agreement is void from the start. There is no election to make, and section 66 handles restoration.",question:"Which factor caused the consent?",branches:[{cond:"coercion, fraud, misrepresentation",label:"Voidable (s 19)",note:"she elects: rescind, or affirm and be put in the true position"},{cond:"mutual mistake of essential fact",label:"Void (s 21)",note:"no election; s 66 restoration"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-vitiating-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Capacity under s 11, and an unlawful object or consideration under s 24, are separate ways to fail.",h:"Capacity, and lawful objects",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two more requirements sit alongside free consent, and both come from section 10."},{t:"p",text:"Section 11 makes a person competent to contract if he is of the age of majority, of sound mind, and not disqualified from contracting by any law. The age of majority is eighteen, under the Age of Majority Act 1971."},{t:"caution",text:"What happens to a minor's agreement in Malaysia is governed by our own statutes and our own authorities. Do not assume the English infancy rules. Check current Malaysian authority instead."},{t:"p",text:"The other requirement is that what the parties are doing must be lawful. Section 24 says when it is not."},{t:"rule",text:"The consideration or object of an agreement is unlawful if it is forbidden by law, or would defeat any law, or is fraudulent, or involves injury to person or property, or the court regards it as immoral or opposed to public policy; every agreement of which the object or consideration is unlawful is void.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 24"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if the purpose or the price of the deal falls into any of those categories, the whole agreement is void."},{t:"example",text:"Two people agree that one will pay the other RM5,000 to withdraw a police report. Whatever they wrote down, that is not a contract any court will enforce. Section 24 makes it void."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not reach for 'illegality' as one big general doctrine. Section 24 lists the categories, and s 25 deals with an agreement that is only partly unlawful. The argument in a real case is about which limb of s 24 is engaged."}]},{id:"sec-l-vitiating-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Name the factor and its section, state the definition, apply it both ways, then say void or voidable.",h:"How to work through it",body:[{t:"p",text:"Name the vitiating factor and its section. State the statutory definition. Apply it to the facts, and argue it both ways where it is arguable."},{t:"p",text:"Then state the consequence — void or voidable — and what follows from that for the remedy your client actually wants. Answers lose their footing in that last step far more often than the first."},{t:"steps",title:"The order to work in",items:[{h:"Name the factor, and its section.",text:"Coercion is s 15, undue influence s 16, fraud s 17, misrepresentation s 18, mistake ss 21 to 23. The section number is part of the answer."},{h:"State the statutory definition.",text:"Quote the Act, not an English formulation of the same idea. The definitions here are statutory and they do not match the English doctrines."},{h:"Apply it to the facts.",text:"Argue it both ways wherever it is genuinely arguable. Most of the marks live in this step."},{h:"State the consequence.",text:"Void, or voidable. This follows from which factor you named, not from how serious the conduct feels."},{h:"Say what follows for the remedy.",text:"Rescission, affirmation with compensation, or restoration under s 66. Answers lose their footing when they stop at the label."}]}]}],quiz:[{id:"qvt1",q:"Both parties are mistaken about a fact essential to their agreement. What is the effect?",options:["The agreement is void","The agreement is voidable","The agreement is valid","The agreement is unenforceable but not void"],answer:0,why:"Section 21 makes it void, so there is nothing to affirm. Voidability under s 19 belongs to coercion, fraud and misrepresentation.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 21 and 19"},{id:"qvt2",q:"Which section defines coercion, and what does it require?",options:["Section 17 — a false suggestion of fact","Section 16 — domination of the will","Section 15 — an act forbidden by the Penal Code, or unlawful detention of property","Section 14 — any improper pressure"],answer:2,why:"The statutory definition is far narrower than 'pressure', and that is where English duress reasoning goes wrong here.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 15"},{id:"qvt3",q:"When does staying silent amount to fraud under s 17?",options:["Always, if a material fact is withheld","Never","Only in contracts of insurance","Where there is a duty to speak, or the silence is equivalent to speech"],answer:3,why:"The explanation to s 17 sets out both limbs. Silence on its own is not fraud.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 17"},{id:"qvt4",q:"What separates misrepresentation under s 18 from fraud under s 17?",options:["The maker believes what he says is true","The statement is about the future","It concerns a third party","It is made in writing"],answer:0,why:"Section 18 covers an assertion not warranted by the information the maker has, though he believes it true.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 17 and 18"},{id:"qvt5",q:"Someone has received a benefit under an agreement discovered to be void. Which section makes him give it back?",options:["Section 24","Section 19","Section 66","Section 74"],answer:2,why:"Section 66 does the work that restitution does in other systems.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 66"},{id:"qvt6",q:"What is the age of majority for capacity to contract?",options:["Twenty-one","Eighteen","Sixteen","Depends on the State"],answer:1,why:"Eighteen, under the Age of Majority Act 1971, read with s 11 of the Contracts Act 1950.",source:"Age of Majority Act 1971; Contracts Act 1950 s 11"}]},{id:"l-remedies",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Breach, damages, and the trouble with an agreed sum",minutes:30,summary:"Section 74 measures the compensation. Section 75 governs a sum the parties named in advance, and the law on that has moved in Malaysia within the last decade.",plants:["ms-rem-01","ms-rem-02","ms-rem-03","ms-rem-04","ms-rem-05"],prepares:["pa-remedies-01"],reading:[{bookId:"cheong-contract-law-malaysia",where:"the chapters on discharge and remedies"},{bookId:"sinnadurai-law-of-contract",where:"for the fuller treatment of s 75 and the Selva Kumar line"}],source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 40, 56, 57, 74, 75; Specific Relief Act 1950 ss 11 and 20; Hadley v Baxendale (1854) 9 Exch 341; Selva Kumar a/l Murugiah v Thiagarajah a/l Retnasamy [1995] 1 MLJ 817 (FC); Cubic Electronics Sdn Bhd v Mars Telecommunications Sdn Bhd [2019] 6 MLJ 15 (FC), also reported at [2019] 2 CLJ 723; Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi [2015] UKSC 67.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Every citation here must be checked in a report you can hold, and you must check whether any Federal Court decision after Cubic Electronics has qualified it. Note on that citation: the corpus previously gave it as [2019] 2 MLJ 806, which was wrong. It is [2019] 6 MLJ 15, also reported at [2019] 2 CLJ 723 and [2019] 2 MLRA 83. The s 75 position has changed once and could change again; this is the paragraph in the whole module most likely to go stale.",plate:"ledger",plateCaption:"The clause against the loss. Section 75 asks what is reasonable, and what counts as reasonable moved once already.",readMinutes:10,sections:[{id:"sec-l-remedies-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 40 lets the innocent party end the contract — but that right is an election and can be lost.",h:"When one side walks away",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Lim pays a RM5,000 deposit for a second-hand van. A week before delivery the seller says he has sold it to somebody else. What can Lim do?"},{t:"p",text:"He has two paths open and he has to pick one. He can treat the contract as over and claim what the breach cost him. Or he can hold the seller to it and keep pressing for the van."},{t:"rule",text:"When a party to a contract has refused to perform, or disabled himself from performing, his promise in its entirety, the promisee may put an end to the contract, unless he has signified by words or conduct his acquiescence in its continuance.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 40"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if the other side refuses to perform at all, you may call the contract off. You lose that right if you have shown that you want the contract to carry on."},{t:"p",text:"The promisee is the person the promise was made to. Ending the contract is an election — a choice, made once, and capable of being lost."},{t:"p",text:"A party who keeps pressing for performance after a repudiation may be held to have affirmed the contract. Affirmation leaves him with damages only."},{t:"p",text:"Time is dealt with by ss 56 and 57. Whether time is of the essence is a question of the parties' intention. A stipulation as to time in a sale of goods or of land is not automatically essential."}]},{id:"sec-l-remedies-1",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 74 is the rule in Hadley v Baxendale, enacted: loss arising naturally, or within contemplation.",h:"How much money: section 74",body:[{t:"p",text:"Damages are meant to put the injured party where performance would have put him. Not better off, and not worse off. Section 74 is where that measure comes from."},{t:"rule",text:"When a contract has been broken, the party who suffers by the breach is entitled to receive compensation for any loss or damage caused to him which naturally arose in the usual course of things from the breach, or which the parties knew, when they made the contract, to be likely to result from the breach. Such compensation is not to be given for any remote and indirect loss or damage.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 74(1) and (2)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you can recover the loss that would ordinarily follow from such a breach. You can also recover unusual loss, but only if the other side knew about the risk when the contract was made. Loss that is remote is not recoverable at all."},{t:"p",text:"Those two limbs will look familiar. This is the rule in *Hadley v Baxendale* (1854) 9 Exch 341, an English Exchequer decision, put into statute. That matters for how you argue it: the starting point is the statutory words, with the English cases as illustration rather than as the source of the rule."},{t:"example",text:"Lim wanted the van for deliveries. Hiring a replacement costs him RM100 a day, and that is ordinary loss under the first limb. Now suppose that when they struck the deal he told the seller he needed the van for one large job worth RM40,000. That loss may fall under the second limb, because the seller knew of it."},{t:"p",text:"The explanation to section 74 adds a practical point. In estimating the loss, the means available for remedying the inconvenience caused by the non-performance must be taken into account. That is the statutory foothold for mitigation."},{t:"example",text:"Mitigation is the duty to take reasonable steps to limit your own loss. If a similar van could be hired down the road, Lim cannot sit still for three months and send the seller the bill."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Lim never mentioned the RM40,000 job when the deal was struck. Can he recover that loss?",options:["Yes — it followed from the breach","No — unusual loss counts only if the seller knew of the risk when the contract was made","Yes, if he tells the seller as soon as the van fails to arrive"],answer:1,why:"The second limb of section 74 turns on what the parties knew when they made the contract. It does not turn on what the seller learns afterwards. The trap is timing. The everyday cost of hiring a replacement is still recoverable under the first limb."}]},{id:"sec-l-remedies-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 75 governs a sum named in the contract, and what counts as reasonable moved with Cubic Electronics.",anchor:"fig-l-remedies-0",h:"A sum named in the contract",body:[{t:"p",text:"Many contracts name a figure in advance. 'If you are late, you pay RM500 for every day.' Is that figure simply payable when the day comes?"},{t:"predict",prompt:"The contract says RM500 for every day the van is late. It arrives six days late. Is the seller simply liable for RM3,000?",options:["Yes — both sides agreed that figure in advance","Only reasonable compensation, and never more than RM3,000","Nothing, because a fixed sum like that is a penalty"],answer:1,reveal:"The named figure is a ceiling, not a bill. Section 75 gives reasonable compensation up to the sum named, so what Lim actually recovers still has to be justified. If you picked the first answer, you read the clause the way the parties wrote it, and in some countries that is what you would get."},{t:"rule",text:"When a contract has been broken, if a sum is named in the contract as the amount to be paid in case of such breach, the party complaining of the breach is entitled to receive from the party who has broken the contract reasonable compensation not exceeding the amount so named.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 75"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the named figure is a ceiling, not an automatic entitlement. What is actually paid is reasonable compensation, up to that figure."},{t:"p",text:"A plaintiff is the person who brings the case. For a long period the Malaysian position on what a plaintiff had to show came from *Selva Kumar a/l Murugiah v Thiagarajah a/l Retnasamy* [1995] 1 MLJ 817 (FC)."},{t:"p",text:"He had to prove actual loss, and he recovered only what he proved, subject to the named sum as a ceiling. The named sum was in effect a cap and nothing more."},{t:"p",text:"That was revisited in *Cubic Electronics Sdn Bhd (in liquidation) v Mars Telecommunications Sdn Bhd* [2019] 2 CLJ 723 (FC). The Federal Court reformulated the approach, drawing on the UK Supreme Court decision in *Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi* [2015] UKSC 67."},{t:"p",text:"Attention now goes to two questions. Does the clause protect a legitimate interest? And is the sum out of all proportion to that interest?"},{t:"caution",text:"This is the single most important 'check before you rely on it' point in the contract module. State the sequence, name both cases, and check whether anything has been decided since. An answer that gives only *Selva Kumar* is out of date, and one that gives only *Cubic Electronics* has not shown it understands what changed."},{t:"diagram",kind:"timeline",title:"Section 75 has moved once, and could again",caption:"An answer giving only one of these two has not shown that it knows the position changed. Check whether anything has been decided since.",alt:"Three points. In 1950 the Contracts Act enacted section 75: reasonable compensation, not exceeding the sum named. In 1995 Selva Kumar held that a plaintiff must prove actual loss, with the named sum as a ceiling. In 2019 Cubic Electronics reformulated the approach around legitimate interest and proportionality to it.",events:[{year:"1950",label:"Contracts Act 1950, s 75 enacted",note:"reasonable compensation, not exceeding the sum named"},{year:"1995",label:"Selva Kumar v Thiagarajah (FC)",note:"a plaintiff must prove actual loss; the named sum is a ceiling"},{year:"2019",label:"Cubic Electronics v Mars (FC)",note:"reformulated: legitimate interest, and proportionality to it",tone:"correct"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-remedies-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Where damages cannot do the job, the Specific Relief Act 1950 governs — not the Contracts Act.",h:"When money is not enough",body:[{t:"p",text:"Some things money cannot replace. A particular piece of land is the standard example, because no two lots are the same."},{t:"p",text:"Damages are the primary remedy. Specific performance — an order compelling actual performance rather than damages — is discretionary, and it comes from a separate statute, the Specific Relief Act 1950."},{t:"p",text:"Section 11 of that Act sets out the cases in which specific performance may be granted. Section 20 sets out those in which it cannot. Two common refusals are where compensation in money is adequate relief, and where the contract would require continuous supervision."},{t:"example",text:"A buyer of a house in Cheras can usually ask the court to order the sale through. A buyer of a hundred bags of cement usually cannot. He can buy cement elsewhere and claim the difference in price."},{t:"caution",text:"The Specific Relief Act is a separate statute with its own scheme. Do not answer a specific performance question out of the Contracts Act, and do not import English equitable discretion in place of the Act's sections."}]},{id:"sec-l-remedies-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Contract, breach, termination or affirmation, measure — and only then any agreed sum.",h:"The order to work in",body:[{t:"p",text:"Establish the contract, then the breach, then whether the innocent party terminated or affirmed, then the measure of loss."},{t:"p",text:"Only then reach any agreed sum. Answers that start with the liquidated damages clause tend to skip the question whether there is a recoverable loss at all."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qrm1",q:"Section 74 of the Contracts Act 1950 puts which familiar rule into statute?",options:["The rule in Hong Kong Fir","The rule in Pinnel's Case","The rule in Carlill","The rule in Hadley v Baxendale"],answer:3,why:"The two limbs — loss arising naturally, and loss within the parties' contemplation — are the statutory form of it.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 74"},{id:"qrm2",q:"Which case reformulated the Malaysian approach to a sum named in the contract under s 75?",options:["Selva Kumar v Thiagarajah","Cubic Electronics v Mars Telecommunications","Kepong Prospecting v Schmidt","Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San"],answer:1,why:"The Federal Court there moved away from the strict requirement of proving actual loss, drawing on Cavendish v Makdessi.",source:"Cubic Electronics Sdn Bhd (in liquidation) v Mars Telecommunications Sdn Bhd [2019] 2 CLJ 723 (FC); Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi [2015] UKSC 67"},{id:"qrm3",q:"Under s 40, what may a promisee do when the other side refuses to perform at all?",options:["Claim the named sum automatically","Seek specific performance only","Nothing until the time for performance passes","Put an end to the contract, unless he has acquiesced in its continuance"],answer:3,why:"Ending the contract is an election, and it can be lost by affirming the contract instead.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 40"},{id:"qrm4",q:"Which statute governs specific performance in Malaysia?",options:["The Contracts Act 1950","The Civil Law Act 1956","The Rules of Court 2012","The Specific Relief Act 1950"],answer:3,why:"The Specific Relief Act 1950 has its own scheme, in ss 11 and 20 among others. Do not answer out of the Contracts Act.",source:"Specific Relief Act 1950"},{id:"qrm5",q:"Why is specific performance a live remedy in a sale of land?",options:["Land contracts are always in writing","The National Land Code requires it","Injunctions are unavailable","Damages are treated as inadequate for land"],answer:3,why:"Whether damages are adequate is the gateway question under the Act, and for land they are treated as inadequate.",source:"Specific Relief Act 1950 ss 11 and 20"},{id:"qrm6",q:"What is the right order of analysis in a remedies question?",options:["Contract, breach, termination or affirmation, measure, then any agreed sum","Specific performance first","Agreed sum first, then loss","Loss first, then contract"],answer:0,why:"Starting at the clause skips the question whether there is a recoverable loss at all.",source:"Standard analytical order for a contract remedies question"}]}];export{e as default};
